When classes extend each other across multiple levels, the resolver walks the whole chain and merges every ancestor's bindings into the final class — `TrackedClickable` here ends up with `isDisabled`, `url`, and `trackingId` all merged together:

```vnr
class Interactive {
    bind isDisabled: "self | attr:disabled";
}

class Clickable extends Interactive {
    bind url: "self | attr:href";
}

class TrackedClickable extends Clickable {
    bind trackingId: "self | attr:data-track-id";
}

reconstruct ".audit-table" -> UiTableListPage {
    pageTitle: "Audit Log";

    child tableRows extends TrackedClickable {
        selector: "tr.audit-row a.audit-link";
    }
}
```

Declare a generic base class first, then extend it with a specialized class that overrides just the `image` binding — the child class's declaration wins over the parent's when both define the same property:

```vnr
class GenericCard {
    bind title: ".title | text";
    bind image: "img | attr:src";
}

class LazyLoadedCard extends GenericCard {
    bind image: "img | attr:data-src";
}

reconstruct "#deals-feed" -> UiModernGridPage {
    pageTitle: "Flash Deals";

    child items extends LazyLoadedCard {
        selector: ".deal-card";
    }
}
```

Declare a base link class, then a thin subclass that only adds `scope: "document";` — any `child` that extends the subclass automatically inherits document-wide scoping without repeating it:

```vnr
class BaseLink {
    bind label: "self | text";
    bind url: "self | attr:href";
}

class DocumentScopedLink extends BaseLink {
    scope: "document";
}

reconstruct "#policy-hub" -> UiSplitLayout {
    pageTitle: "Legal Center";
    bind headline: "h1.hub-title | text";

    child legalLinks extends DocumentScopedLink {
        selector: "#site-footer a.legal-link";
    }
}
```

When the field shapes only coincidentally overlap rather than representing a true "is-a" relationship, it's usually clearer to declare independent sibling classes rather than forcing an `extends` relationship between them — inheritance is for genuine specialization, not just for avoiding a bit of duplication:

```vnr
class EpisodeHost {
    bind name: ".host-name | text";
    bind avatarUrl: ".host-avatar img | attr:src";
    bind bio: ".host-bio | text";
}

class EpisodeGuest {
    bind name: ".guest-name | text";
    bind avatarUrl: ".guest-avatar img | attr:src";
    bind bio: ".guest-bio | text";
    bind episodeCount: ".guest-stats .episodes | text | number";
}
```

Why does this fail to compile?

```vnr
class A extends B {
    bind x: "self | text";
}

class B extends A {
    bind y: "self | text";
}
```

The problem: `A` extends `B`, and `B` extends `A` — the resolver detects this as circular inheritance (`A -> B -> A`) while building the ancestry graph, which it can never fully resolve, so it fails at compile time. Break the cycle by having both extend a shared, non-circular base instead:

```vnr
class Base {
    bind x: "self | text";
    bind y: "self | text";
}

class A extends Base {
    bind extraA: "self | attr:data-a";
}

class B extends Base {
    bind extraB: "self | attr:data-b";
}
```

Why does this fail?

```vnr
class Derived extends MissingBase {
    bind extra: "self | attr:data-extra";
}
```

The problem: `MissingBase` is never declared anywhere the compiler can find it — in single-file linter mode it must live in the same file or a sibling file in the same directory; in a full workspace compile it must be declared somewhere in the compiled tree. Declare the missing base class before (or after — order doesn't matter in a full compile) the class that extends it:

```vnr
class MissingBase {
    bind z: "self | text";
}

class Derived extends MissingBase {
    bind extra: "self | attr:data-extra";
}
```

Why does this fail to compile?

```vnr
selector "#promo-ribbon" -> UiTagBadge {
    label: "Limited Time";
}
```

The problem: every `selector` block requires an `action` key (`hide` or `replace`) — without it, the compiler doesn't know whether to just hide the legacy element or mount a component in its place. Add the missing key:

```vnr
selector "#promo-ribbon" -> UiTagBadge {
    action: replace;
    label: "Limited Time";
}
```

Why does this fail to compile?

```vnr
child orphanList {
    selector: ".x";
}
```

The problem: `child` is only valid when nested inside a `selector` or `reconstruct` block — it has no meaning at the top level of a file, since it needs a parent component to attach its resulting array prop to. Nest it inside a block that mounts a component:

```vnr
reconstruct "#page" -> UiGridPage {
    child orphanList {
        selector: ".x";
    }
}
```

Why does this fail?

```vnr
reconstruct "#page" -> UiDashboardPage {
    child items {
        selector: ".a";
    }
    child items {
        selector: ".b";
    }
}
```

The problem: two `child` blocks share the name `items` at the same nesting level under the same parent, which creates a naming conflict — each child list needs a distinct prop name so the resolver knows which array each one maps to. Give each list its own name:

```vnr
reconstruct "#page" -> UiDashboardPage {
    child primaryItems {
        selector: ".a";
    }
    child secondaryItems {
        selector: ".b";
    }
}
```

My `preserve` block compiled without any errors, but the legacy chat widget just disappeared — it never reappeared anywhere on the page. What went wrong?

```vnr
reconstruct "#support-center" -> UiItemDetailsPage {
    preserve {
        liveChatSlot: "#zendesk-chat-widget";
    }
}
```

Global CSS stylesheet rules must be defined inside a `customStyles` block with braces (curly brackets) containing one or more string literals, rather than as a scalar key-value pair.

Here is an example:
```vnr
theme "ModernDark" {
    variables {
        --spm-accent: "#7c6af5";
    }
    customStyles {
        "body { background-color: #121212 !important; }"
        "#ad-banner { display: none !important; }"
    }
}
```

To map a list of items (like a row in a table or a card in a grid) to relative child properties, define a `class` block specifying a custom class name, define its `scope` selector, and then extend that class in a `child` block inside your `reconstruct` or `selector` declaration.

Here is an example:
```vnr
class GithubIssueItem {
    scope: ".js-issue-row";
    bind issueNumber: "span.opened-by | text | cleanNumber";
    bind title: "a.markdown-title | text";
    bind commentsCount: "a.Link--muted | text | number";
}

reconstruct "#issues-container" -> UiTableListPage {
    pageTitle: "Repository Issues";

    child tableRows extends GithubIssueItem {
        selector: ".js-issue-row";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec (.vnr) code to reconstruct the discussion area while preserving interactive legacy inputs:

```vnr
reconstruct "#discussion-thread" -> UiPostDetails {
    pageTitle: "Discussion Thread";

    preserve {
        commentFormSlot: "#legacy-comment-editor";
        profileCardSlot: "#member-card-widget";
    }
}
```

You can extract space-separated data tags into a JSON array using the `split` pipe operation. The default behavior of `split` without arguments splits by space:

```vnr
class GalleryCard {
    bind tags: "self | attr:data-tags | split";
    bind viewUrl: "a.preview-link | attr:href";
    bind imageUrl: "img | attr:src";
}

reconstruct ".gallery-item-container" -> UiModernGridPage {
    pageTitle: "Wallpaper Gallery";

    child items extends GalleryCard {
        selector: ".gallery-item";
    }
}
```

You can specify a custom delimiter by passing arguments to the `split` pipe, like `split:' > '`:

```vnr
class CategoryBreadcrumb {
    bind pathSegments: "span.hierarchy | text | split:' > '";
}
```

You can parse digits and float values from text segments containing extra characters using the `number` and `cleanNumber` extractor pipes:

```vnr
class MetadataCard {
    bind count: "span.item-count | text | number";
    bind rating: "span.item-rating | text | number";
    bind price: "span.item-price | text | cleanNumber";
}
```

By default, bindings look inside the matched container block. To fetch properties from elements physically outside the matched container, use `scope: "document";`:

```vnr
selector "#legacy-sidebar" -> UiStatsDashboard {
    action: replace;

    bind currentUser: {
        scope: "document";
        selector: "#top-bar span.user-name-display | text";
    }
}
```

You can constrain a `reconstruct` block to trigger only on pages matching a specific URL regex pattern using the `urlPattern` property and a C++ raw string literal to avoid backslash escape overhead:

```vnr
reconstruct "#gallery-feed-wrapper" -> UiModernGridPage {
    urlPattern: R"(\/gallery\/\d+)";
    pageTitle: "Image Gallery Feed";

    child items {
        selector: ".thumbnail-card";
        bind imageUrl: "img | attr:src";
    }
}
```

Here is the `.vnr` code to reconstruct the HTML data-table into a React-based `UiTableListPage`:

```vnr
class UserRow {
    bind name: "a.username | text";
    bind profileUrl: "a.username | attr:href";
    bind role: "span.label | text";
    bind lastSeen: "time | attr:datetime";
}

reconstruct "table.data-table" -> UiTableListPage {
    pageTitle: "User Accounts Directory";

    child tableRows extends UserRow {
        selector: "tr.user-record";
    }
}
```

You can perform in-place replacements using `selector` blocks with `action: replace;`:

```vnr
selector ".video-preview-widget" -> UiImageViewer {
    action: replace;
    imageSrc: "self | attr:data-poster-url";
    videoId: "self | attr:data-video-id | number";
}
```

Use a `theme` block with nested `variables` and `customStyles` blocks:

```vnr
theme "ThemeDarkDefault" {
    variables {
        --spm-bg-primary: "#0b0c10";
        --spm-bg-secondary: "#1f2833";
        --spm-accent: "#66fcf1";
        --spm-text-primary: "#c5c6c7";
    }
    customStyles {
        "body { font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif !important; }"
        ".ad-space { display: none !important; }"
    }
}
```

Use the `extends` keyword in your class declarations:

```vnr
class BaseItem {
    bind id: "self | attr:data-item-id | number";
    bind title: "a.title-link | text";
    bind url: "a.title-link | attr:href";
}

class ClassifiedItem extends BaseItem {
    bind price: ".price-tag | text | cleanNumber";
    bind location: ".location | text";
}

class JobItem extends BaseItem {
    bind company: ".company-name | text";
    bind remote: ".remote-tag | text";
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec (.vnr) code to reconstruct the blog grid into a `UiModernGridPage` targeting the `#posts-feed` container:

```vnr
class BlogCard {
    bind title: "h2.post-title a | text";
    bind url: "h2.post-title a | attr:href";
    bind imageUrl: ".thumb-container img | attr:src";
    bind category: "self | attr:data-category";
    bind publishedAt: "span.meta-date | text";
}

reconstruct "#posts-feed" -> UiModernGridPage {
    pageTitle: "Blog Feed";

    child items extends BlogCard {
        selector: ".k-post-card";
    }
}
```

You can reconstruct this product listing using `UiTableListPage`. We will clean the currency prefix and thousands separator using the `cleanNumber` pipe operation:

```vnr
class ProductRow {
    bind title: "a.item-link | text";
    bind url: "a.item-link | attr:href";
    bind imageUrl: "img.lazy-load | attr:src";
    bind price: "span.price-label | text | cleanNumber";
    bind status: "span.badge-status | text";
}

reconstruct ".listing-container" -> UiTableListPage {
    pageTitle: "Search Results";

    child tableRows extends ProductRow {
        selector: ".search-item-row";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec block to replace the legacy header with `UiNavHeader`:

```vnr
selector "#global-header" -> UiNavHeader {
    action: replace;
    logoUrl: ".header-logo-wrap img | attr:src";
    logoHref: ".header-logo-wrap a | attr:href";

    child primaryLinks {
        scope: "container";
        selector: "nav.navigation-links a.nav-item";
        bind label: "self | text";
        bind url: "self | attr:href";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to replace the featured media block in place with `UiImageCard`:

```vnr
selector ".featured-media-block" -> UiImageCard {
    action: replace;
    bind imageUrl: "img.featured-img | attr:src";
    bind caption: "p.media-caption | text";
    bind mediaId: "self | attr:data-media-id | number";
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the overview into `UiDashboardPage`, using two separate `child` blocks for the metrics and the activity feed:

```vnr
reconstruct "#admin-overview" -> UiDashboardPage {
    pageTitle: "Support Overview";

    child metrics {
        selector: ".metric-tile";
        bind label: ".metric-label | text";
        bind value: ".metric-value | text";
    }

    child recentActivity {
        selector: ".activity-row";
        bind actor: ".activity-actor | text";
        bind action: ".activity-action | text";
        bind timestamp: ".activity-time | attr:datetime";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the two-column article layout into `UiSplitLayout`, preserving the legacy sidebar widget in its own slot:

```vnr
reconstruct "#article-layout" -> UiSplitLayout {
    pageTitle: "Article";

    bind headline: "h1.headline | text";
    bind bodyHtml: ".body-copy | html";

    preserve {
        relatedLinksSlot: "#legacy-related-links-widget";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to replace the changelog box with a scrollable `UiScrollPanel`, mapping each entry as a child item:

```vnr
selector "#release-notes-list" -> UiScrollPanel {
    action: replace;

    child entries {
        selector: ".changelog-entry";
        bind version: ".version-tag | text";
        bind description: ".entry-text | text";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to replace the status flag in place with `UiTagBadge`:

```vnr
selector ".order-status-flag" -> UiTagBadge {
    action: replace;
    bind label: "self | text";
    bind status: "self | attr:data-status";
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the hero section into `UiHeroLanding`, using a small reusable class for the nav links:

```vnr
class HeroNavLink {
    bind label: "self | text";
    bind url: "self | attr:href";
}

reconstruct "#homepage-hero" -> UiHeroLanding {
    tagline: "Build Faster, Ship Sooner";
    subtext: "The toolkit for modern engineering teams.";
    ctaLabel: "Get Started";
    ctaUrl: "https://example.com/signup";

    child primaryLinks extends HeroNavLink {
        selector: "a.hero-nav-link";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the listing into `UiItemDetailsPage`, cleaning the price with `cleanNumber` and preserving the seller contact form:

```vnr
reconstruct "#book-listing" -> UiItemDetailsPage {
    pageTitle: "Book Details";

    bind title: "h1.book-title | text";
    bind price: ".book-price | text | cleanNumber";
    bind condition: ".book-condition | text";

    preserve {
        contactSellerSlot: "#legacy-seller-contact-form";
    }
}
```

Since the pager sits outside the matched `#forum-results` container, use `scope: "document"` on the `child` block so its selector queries the whole page instead of just the container's descendants:

```vnr
reconstruct "#forum-results" -> UiPaginationBar {
    pageTitle: "Forum Results";

    child pages {
        scope: "document";
        selector: "#global-forum-pager a.pager-link";
        bind label: "self | text";
        bind url: "self | attr:href";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the question page into `UiPostDetails`, with the answers mapped as a `child` array:

```vnr
reconstruct "#question-detail" -> UiPostDetails {
    pageTitle: "Question Detail";

    bind title: "h1.question-title | text";
    bind bodyHtml: ".question-body | html";

    child answers {
        selector: ".answer-row";
        bind body: ".answer-body | text";
        bind votes: ".answer-votes | text | number";
    }
}
```

Here is the Veneer Spec code to reconstruct the revenue widget into `UiStatsDashboard`, using `cleanNumber` to strip the currency symbol and thousands separator:

```vnr
reconstruct "#revenue-widget" -> UiStatsDashboard {
    pageTitle: "Revenue";

    child metrics {
        selector: ".metric-tile";
        bind label: ".metric-label | text";
        bind value: ".metric-value | text | cleanNumber";
    }
}
```

You can gate a `reconstruct` block so it only mounts under a specific viewport size using the `mediaQuery` property, set to a standard CSS media query string:

```vnr
reconstruct "#mobile-category-drawer" -> UiGridPage {
    mediaQuery: "(max-width: 768px)";
    pageTitle: "Categories";

    child categoryLinks {
        selector: "a.drawer-link";
        bind label: "self | text";
        bind url: "self | attr:href";
    }
}
```

You can nest `child` blocks inside one another to map recursive structures like threaded replies — each level of nesting produces its own array-valued prop scoped to its parent:

```vnr
reconstruct "#post-thread" -> UiPostDetails {
    pageTitle: "Post Thread";

    child topLevelComments {
        selector: ".comment.depth-0";
        bind body: "p.comment-text | text";
        bind commentId: "self | attr:data-comment-id";

        child replies {
            selector: ".comment.depth-1";
            bind body: "p.comment-text | text";
            bind commentId: "self | attr:data-comment-id";
        }
    }
}
```

Use the `hiddenInputs` extractor to collect every `<input type="hidden">` descendant as a JSON array, so the new component can still submit compatible hidden values:

```vnr
selector "#legacy-quick-search" -> UiSearchBar {
    action: replace;
    placeholder: "Search products";
    submitUrl: "https://example.com/search";
    queryParamName: "q";
    bind hiddenFields: "self | hiddenInputs";
}
```

The `hrefOrOnclick` extractor normalizes both a real `href` attribute and an inline `onclick` navigation handler into a single destination string, so you don't need separate logic for each case:

```vnr
selector ".product-tile" -> UiImageCard {
    action: replace;
    bind imageUrl: "img | attr:src";
    bind caption: ".tile-caption | text";
    bind linkUrl: ".tile-caption | hrefOrOnclick";
    bind sku: "self | attr:data-sku";
}
```

When a label and its value are adjacent siblings instead of being nested together, match the label element and pipe through `nextSiblingText` to grab the following element's text content:

```vnr
reconstruct "#status-panel" -> UiStatsDashboard {
    pageTitle: "System Status";

    child metrics {
        selector: ".stat-row";
        bind label: ".stat-label | text";
        bind value: ".stat-label | nextSiblingText";
    }
}
```

The `selector` base extractor, applied to `self`, generates a unique CSS selector string for the matched element itself — useful for features like scroll-position restoration that need to re-locate the original node later:

```vnr
reconstruct "#results-pager" -> UiPaginationBar {
    pageTitle: "Search Results";

    child pages {
        selector: "a.page-link";
        bind label: "self | text";
        bind url: "self | attr:href";
        bind anchorSelector: "self | selector";
    }
}
```

You can preserve as many legacy widgets as needed in one `preserve` block — just add one named slot per widget:

```vnr
reconstruct "#item-page" -> UiItemDetailsPage {
    pageTitle: "Item Details";

    bind title: "h1.item-name | text";
    bind description: ".item-desc | text";

    preserve {
        qnaSlot: "#legacy-qna-widget";
        shippingCalculatorSlot: "#legacy-shipping-calc";
    }
}
```

Why does this fail to compile?

```vnr
selector "#footer-links" -> UiNavHeader {
    action: replace
    logoHref: "https://example.com/";
}
```

Every property assignment in Veneer Spec must terminate with a semicolon — the `action: replace` line is missing its trailing `;`, which the compiler needs to know the statement has ended:

```vnr
selector "#footer-links" -> UiNavHeader {
    action: replace;
    logoHref: "https://example.com/";
}
```

A Veneer Spec project may only declare a single `theme` block across the entire compiled workspace, even if the two declarations live in different files — the compiler treats it as one global project-wide theme, not a per-file setting. Consolidate both into one declaration:

```vnr
// theme.vnr — single, consolidated theme declaration for the whole workspace
theme "StorefrontDefault" {
    variables {
        --spm-accent: "#e11d48";
    }
    customStyles {
        "#legacy-popup-ad { display: none !important; }"
    }
}
```

Wrap the JSON array in a raw string literal so it doesn't need any backslash or quote escaping, then assign it directly to the static prop:

```vnr
reconstruct "#catalog-grid" -> UiGridPage {
    pageTitle: "Catalog";
    tagGroups: R"([
      { "title": "Category", "typeKey": "category" },
      { "title": "Brand", "typeKey": "brand" },
      { "title": "Price", "typeKey": "price" }
    ])";
}
```

Explain what the following Veneer Spec (.vnr) code does:

```vnr
// ❌ Will NOT parse as JSON — the escaped quotes make this a plain string containing braces
badExample: "{\"a\": 1}";

// ✅ Correct approach — use a raw string so the JSON body is unescaped
goodExample: R"({"a": 1})";
```

This snippet is an example of **Arrays and objects only coerce from **raw strings**, not from unescaped quoted strings** in Veneer Spec.

```vnr
// ❌ Will NOT parse as JSON — the escaped quotes make this a plain string containing braces
badExample: "{\"a\": 1}";

// ✅ Correct approach — use a raw string so the JSON body is unescaped
goodExample: R"({"a": 1})";
```

When compiled with `spm-cli`, this is lexed, parsed into an AST, resolved (class inheritance and scoping applied), and emitted into the target `manifest.json`, following the rules for this construct described in the Veneer Spec language reference.

